{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:59:27.106021", "benchmark": "gaia2", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia2_scenario_universe_21_rxk1cz", "task_desc": "I ran into Mr. Winfrey today, and since it’s his birthday coming up, I want to get him the JanSport Lunch Break in Navy Blue, so please buy one of them. We also talked about checking out the Lund Luxe property together sometime, so just save it to my saved properties for now. While we were catching up, I realized my inbox is a mess—please delete all the emails I got on August 8th this year to clean things up. Also, add Mr. Winfrey as a contact so I can stay in touch, his email address is winwin2", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "I'll break down this task into sub-requests:\n1. Buy a JanSport Lunch Break in Navy Blue\n2. Save the Lund Luxe property to saved properties\n3. Delete all emails received on August 8th this year\n4. Add Mr. Winfrey as a contact (email: winwin29@gaia2mail.com)\n5. Check for any missed relevant information\n\nLet me start by gathering information in parallel.\nI found the product and property. The email search by \"August 8\" returned nothing, so I need to search differently. Let me also check the product details to confirm the Navy Blue variant, and search emails by date range.\n\nThe JanSport Lunch Break Navy Blue variant is available (item_id: e402a19d6a0047ba8430f241e2f00462). Let me proceed with the actions and search emails more thoroughly.\nThe quantity and offset need to be integers. Let me retry those. The property was saved and contact was added. Let me list emails with proper integer types.\nThe tool is receiving the quantity as a string. Let me try without specifying quantity (it defaults to 1) and list emails without offset.\nThe cart item was added. The email list tool is having type issues with the limit parameter. Let me try with just the folder name.\nThe list works without the limit parameter. I need to find emails from August 8th. The timestamp 1728827076.34 corresponds to a date. Let me check the current user's email and get more emails to find the August 8th ones. Let me list more emails and also check the current user details.\nThe list_emails seems to return only a limited set. I need to find emails from August 8th. The timestamp 1728827076.34 = let me compute: that's around October 13, 2024. I need to find August 8th emails specifically. Let me search emails with different queries and check the full list. Let me try searching by date-related terms and also look at the full inbox.\n\nLet me try searching with various queries to find August 8th emails.\nThe search only matches content/subject/sender/recipients, not dates. I need to find emails by their actual date. The list_emails returns emails but I need to see all of them with their timestamps. The list seems truncated. Let me try to get the full list by reading emails by index to understand the structure, and find which emails are from August 8th.\n\nLet me read emails by index to find the August 8th ones. First, let me understand how many emails there are.\nThe integer type issue affects these tools. The list_emails without limit worked and returned emails with timestamps. Let me use list_emails (which worked) to get the full inbox and examine timestamps. The issue is the tool seems to return a fixed set. Let me call list_emails again to see if it returns more, and check the timestamps to identify August 8th emails.\n\nActually, the list_emails returned a truncated view. Let me think about the timestamps. 1728827076.34 = October 13, 2024. I need August 8, 2024 = 1723075200 to 1723161599.\n\nLet me get the full email list. The list_emails tool returned only 2 emails in the result shown (truncated display). Let me try calling it again to see the complete list.\nThe list_emails is returning a truncated view (only showing 2 emails in the display, possibly more in the full result). The display is being cut off. I need to see the full list with all timestamps. The tool seems to have a display limit. Let me try the search_emails with different terms to surface August 8th emails, or try reading individual emails by ID.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The list_emails returns emails but the display truncates. I need the email_ids and timestamps. Let me try searching with common terms that might be in August 8th emails. But that's unreliable.\n\nLet me try get_email_by_index with integer - the issue is the tool wrapper. Let me retry with the idx as a plain number without quotes in a different way. Actually the error says it got a string. The function schema requires string type for these. This is a tool limitation.\n\nLet me try list_emails with offset as integer - but it also requires string. Hmm. The list_emails worked WITHOUT offset/limit. So the default returned a set. Maybe the default returns ALL emails but the display is truncated in the output.\n\nLet me look more carefully - the result shows \"emails\": [...] with 2 entries visible but truncated. There may be more. Let me try to get emails by searching for specific content. Actually, let me reconsider - maybe I should search for emails from August 8 by trying date-based search terms that might appear in content.\n\nActually, a better approach: the list_emails default may return all emails. The display just truncated the JSON. Let me try to paginate using offset. The offset param failed as string. But maybe I can pass it differently. Let me retry list_emails with offset to get later emails.\n\nWait - the error was \"Argument 'offset' must be of type <class 'int'>\". This means the tool implementation expects int but the schema says string. There may be no way to pass int through this interface. \n\nLet me instead try get_email_by_index - same issue. \n\nAlternative: search_emails works with string query. Let me search for terms likely in August 8 emails. But I don't know the content.\n\nLet me reconsider the list_emails output. It returned 2 emails but the JSON might contain more that got cut in display. Let me try a very targeted search. Actually, let me re-examine: the first list_emails call returned the same 2 emails. So the default view shows the 2 most recent (Oct 13). \n\nI think the real issue is I need to access older emails. Let me try the offset parameter again but the tool rejects strings. \n\nHmm, let me try passing offset as a number value (not string) in the JSON. The schema says string but maybe the actual call can take int. Let me try.</think:6124c78e>The list_emails default view shows only the 2 most recent emails (Oct 13). I need to access August 8th emails. The offset/limit parameters are rejecting string types. Let me try passing them as actual integers in the JSON.\nThe tool strictly requires integer types which the interface passes as strings. Let me try the get_email_by_index with a numeric value to see if that path works differently.\nThese tools require integer arguments that the interface can't pass. Let me work with what works: list_emails (default) and search_emails. The default list_emails returned 2 emails (both Oct 13). I need to find August 8th emails. Let me search with various content queries that might surface them, and also check other folders (SENT, DRAFT, TRASH).\n\nLet me search broadly and check other folders.\nThe SENT folder has emails but the display is truncated. The TRASH and DRAFT are empty. The INBOX default view only shows the 2 most recent. I need to find August 8th emails in INBOX.\n\nThe list_emails default seems to return a limited window. The display truncates the JSON. Let me think - the INBOX returned 2 emails both from Oct 13. But there could be many more emails with earlier dates that aren't shown because the display truncates.\n\nThe key problem: I can't pass integer offsets, and the display truncates. But search_emails works. Let me search for content that would be in August 8th emails. I don't know the content though.\n\nLet me reconsider. The task says \"delete all the emails I got on August 8th this year.\" The emails I \"got\" = received = INBOX. I need to identify INBOX emails from Aug 8, 2024.\n\nThe list_emails default returned 2 emails (Oct 13). Maybe the default returns only the latest few. Let me try search_emails with date-related content. Actually, let me try searching for common email subjects. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the list_emails default returns ALL emails but the JSON display is just truncated in my view. The result showed 2 emails but maybe there are more in the full JSON that got cut off. Let me look - the first email is complete, the second is truncated mid-content (\"As a mechanic, I've recen\"). So the display cut off. There might be more emails after.\n\nI really need to see the full list. Let me try a differen", "expected": [{"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 0.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-944d8844-fa62-4890-9e22-c19593c37a48", "dependencies": ["Event-USER-e2a09c56-62c8-441c-b24a-320a6f8daf77"], "event_relative_time": 1.0, "action": {"action_id": "", "app": "Emails", "function": "delete_email", "operation_type": null, "args": [{"name": "email_id", "value": "553ce23eef6b49629d7cfa27b25cd3a6", "value_type": "str"}, {"name": "folder_name", "value": "INBOX", "value_type": "str"}]}, "metadata": null}, {"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 0.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-486f3a9c-4183-44a8-a7e0-135a6250a2d0", "dependencies": ["Event-USER-e2a09c56-62c8-441c-b24a-320a6f8daf77"], "event_relative_time": 1.0, "action": {"action_id": "", "app": "Emails", "function": "delete_email", "operation_type": null, "args": [{"name": "email_id", "value": "c66871937cb94f5c87eac3caeccf833c", "value_type": "str"}, {"name": "folder_name", "value": "INBOX", "value_type": "str"}]}, "metadata": null}, {"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 0.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-5da1d39a-101d-4349-ad96-36ccbad899b2", "dependencies": ["