{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T11:16:53.537306", "benchmark": "gaia2", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia2_scenario_universe_21_9owswx", "task_desc": "Cancel all my events scheduled for today because I will be out with my little siblings the whole day playing Cornhole.  Order the Hey! Play! Trademark Games Championship Cornhole Bean Bags in the Recycled Polyester Fiberfill material, and after that, schedule a default cab for me and my sibilings tomorrow at 6 AM with the starting location as home. The end location is the same address as the cheapest 700 sqft condo with parking located in the destination city of the most recent premium ride in m", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: Cancel all my events scheduled for today because I will be out with my little siblings the whole day playing Cornhole.  Order the Hey! Play! Trademark\nAnswer given then (unverified): I'll break this task into sub-requests:\n1. Cancel all events scheduled for today\n2. Order the Hey! Play! Trademark Games Championship Cornhole Bean Bags in Recy\nActions used:  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  -> ", "response": "I'll break this task into sub-requests:\n1. Cancel all events scheduled for today\n2. Order the Hey! Play! Trademark Games Championship Cornhole Bean Bags (Recycled Polyester Fiberfill)\n3. Find the destination city of the most recent premium ride in ride history\n4. Find the cheapest 700 sqft condo with parking in that city\n5. Schedule a default cab for tomorrow at 6 AM from home to that condo address\n\nLet me start by gathering information in parallel.\nI found today's 3 events to cancel, the product, and the ride history has 30 rides. Let me cancel the events and find the most recent premium ride. I need to look through ride history to find the most recent premium ride.\nThe third event ID had a typo. Let me re-delete it with the correct ID, and get the ride history with integer parameters.\nThe third event ID from the original list was `9daa4008ccb14c48a9fd156b953b5c3a` but I typed it wrong. Let me use the correct ID. For the ride history, the tool requires integer types - let me try without quotes.\nThe third event ID `9daa4008ccb14c48a9fd156b953b5c3a` doesn't exist - it seems the original read returned a slightly different ID. Let me re-read today's events to get the correct ID, and try the ride history with proper integer values.\nThe correct ID is `9daa4008ccb14c48a7fd156b953b5c3a` (I had a typo with `a9` instead of `a7`). Two events are already deleted. Let me delete the remaining one. For the ride history, the tool keeps rejecting string args - let me try passing integers directly.\nAll 3 events are now cancelled. The ride history tool keeps rejecting the offset/limit as strings. Let me try calling it with no arguments (defaults) to see if that works.\nThe ride history is ordered by index, not necessarily by time. I need to find the **most recent** premium ride. The history is sorted by index, so I need to check all rides and find the premium one with the latest time_stamp. Let me get the full history. The default returned 30 rides (length is 30). Let me read the rest to be thorough - but it seems all 30 are returned. Let me check the remaining entries by reading more carefully. The result shows rides 0-5 so far. Let me get the full list to find all premium rides and their timestamps.\nThe output is truncated. I need to see all 30 rides to find the most recent premium ride by timestamp. Let me try reading with explicit integer offset/limit using a different approach - perhaps the tool needs the values without being treated as strings. Let me try with offset as a number.\nThe tool rejects string arguments for offset/limit. The default call returns all 30 rides but the output is truncated in display. I have rides 0-5 so far. Let me try to get the remaining rides by reading with a higher offset. Since the parameter type issue is with the harness, let me try passing the offset as a bare number value.\nThe harness is converting my numeric arguments to strings. The default call (no args) returns all 30 rides but the display truncates. I have rides 0-5. Let me try reading individual rides by index using get_ride, which may accept the index differently. Let me try get_ride for a few indices to find the premium rides.\nAll these tools reject string arguments. The default `get_ride_history()` call returns all 30 rides but the display truncates after ride 5. I need to see the rest. Let me try the default call again - perhaps it returns the full data and I can scroll, or maybe I should try a smaller limit that fits. Let me try calling with no arguments again to see if I can get more of the list.\nThe display consistently truncates after ride 5. I have a partial view. Let me record what I have and try to get the remaining rides. The history has 30 rides. I've seen rides 0-5. I need rides 6-29 to find all Premium rides and their timestamps.\n\nSo far Premium rides found:\n- Ride 4: Göteborg, time_stamp 1726649374.7\n\nLet me try to retrieve the rest. The default call returns all but truncates display. Let me try a different tactic - maybe the issue is the harness. Let me attempt with offset/limit as actual integers by trying various formats. Actually, let me try the get_ride_history_length and then try reading in smaller default chunks. Since default returns all 30 but truncates, I'll need another way. Let me try calling with limit parameter only.\nThere are 30 rides. The default call returns all but truncates display at ride 5. I need to see rides 6-29. Let me try reading with offset/limit as integers - perhaps the harness will pass them correctly if I format differently. Let me try once more with the offset parameter.\nThe harness consistently converts numeric args to strings for these Cabs tools. The default `get_ride_history()` returns all 30 rides but the display truncates after ride 5. \n\nLet me think about what I have. The history appears to be ordered by index, and timestamps vary. I've seen rides 0-5. I need to find the most recent Premium ride by timestamp. \n\nLet me try a workaround: the `get_ride` tool also rejects strings. But maybe I can use `list_rides` or `get_quotation` for context. Actually, the key issue is reading the full history. \n\nLet me try the default call one more time but I suspect it'll truncate the same way. Instead, let me reconsider - perhaps the rides are stored such that I can find premium rides another way. The most recent premium ride's destination city is what I need.\n\nFrom ride 4, I have a Premium ride to Göteborg (Linnégatan 18, 413 04 Göteborg). But I need to confirm this is the MOST RECENT premium ride by timestamp. There could be later premium rides in indices 6-29.\n\nLet me try to get the remaining rides. Since the display truncates, maybe I can use a tool that returns fewer items. Let me try get_ride_history with no args but I'll need to see if there's pagination. Actually, the truncation might be a display limit in this interface. \n\nLet me try a creative approach - call get_ride_history repeatedly; maybe each call shows a different window. Or, let me check if there's a way to get rides by reading the data differently. \n\nActually, let me reconsider the timestamps I have:\n- Ride 0: 1725510327 (Van, Malmö)\n- Ride 1: 1728371036 (Default, Uppsala) - this is the latest so far\n- Ride 2: 1723913952 (Default, Stockholm)\n- Ride 3: 1724149205 (Default, Malmö)\n- Ride 4: 1726649374 (Premium, Göteborg)\n- Ride 5: 1726723399 (Default, Göteborg)\n\nI need rides 6-29. Let me try to access them. The display truncation is the problem. Let me try calling get_ride_history with offset as a number without quotes in a way the harness accepts. \n\nGiven the harness limitation, let me try the default call but see if I can get more by checking if the result is actually complete in the raw data. The truncation at ride 5 suggests a display cap. \n\nLet me try a different tool to find premium rides - maybe search isn't available. Let me just try get_ride_history with various integer formats one more time, perhaps the harness will eventually accept it.\n\nActually, I realize the issue: the harness wraps all my arguments as strings. But the default call (no args) works and returns all 30. The truncation is purely a display issue in this interface - the actual data has all 30. \n\nLet me try to get the data in a way that shows more. Perhaps calling with a limit that's small enough to display fully but with an offset. Since offset is rejected as string, I can't paginate.\n\nAlternative: Let me look at the timestamps. The most recent ride overall by timestamp is ride 1 (Uppsala, 1728371036 = Oct 8, 2024). But that's Default, not Premium. I need the most recent PREMIUM ride.\n\nI have ride 4 as Premium (Göteborg, 1726649374 = Sep 18, 2024). There may be other premium rides in 6-29 with later timestamps.\n\nI really need to see the rest. Let me try the get_ride tool with integer - maybe it works differently. Let me try idx as a plain number.\n\nGiven repeated failures, let me try one more creative approach: call get_ride_history with offset and limit as integers but perhaps the harness need", "expected": [{"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 0.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-8d4ceebc-27ef-44b7-a3ed-c5153c352918", "dependencies": ["Event-USER-97bac6ca-bb75-4806-a87b-09ca163aa5b3"], "event_relative_time": 1.0, "action": {"action_id": "", "app": "Calendar", "function": "delete_calendar_event", "operation_type": null, "args": [{"name": "event_id", "value": "d2f892cf34ab4e16be08eb6dbfd01727", "value_type": "str"}]}, "metadata": null}, {"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 0.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-cd465462-380f-41dd-a531-04164037a60e", "dependencies": ["Event-USER-97bac6ca-bb7