{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T11:20:37.365062", "benchmark": "gaia2", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia2_scenario_universe_22_3m9r7l", "task_desc": "Delete the calendar event on October 20, 2024 and reschedule all of my calendar events on October 18, 2024 to October 20, 2024 with the same details as before. Send a group email to all of my contacts in Portland, United States to invite them to an all day wine tasting event on October 18, 2024 at my home. Schedule this into my calendar from 08:00:00 to 20:00:00, tagged as personal and the location set to home.", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: Delete the calendar event on October 20, 2024 and reschedule all of my calendar events on October 18, 2024 to October 20, 2024 with the same details a\nAnswer given then (unverified): I'll break this task into sub-requests:\n1. Delete the calendar event on October 20, 2024\n2. Reschedule all calendar events on October 18, 2024 to October 20, 20\nActions used:  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  -> ", "response": "I'll break this task into sub-requests:\n1. Delete the calendar event on October 20, 2024\n2. Reschedule all calendar events on October 18, 2024 to October 20, 2024 (same details)\n3. Find all contacts in Portland, United States\n4. Send a group email inviting them to an all-day wine tasting on October 18, 2024 at my home\n5. Schedule the wine tasting event in calendar (Oct 18, 08:00-20:00, tag=personal, location=home)\n\nLet me start by inspecting the calendar events and contacts.\nLet me get the full list of events on Oct 20 and all contacts. Let me page through contacts and get the Oct 20 events.\nThe Oct 20 event to delete is \"Wine List Review\" (event_id: 20069a91f74c480eba3adeed1ff252c1). Let me fix the contacts offset type and get the rest of the contacts.\nThe offset needs to be an integer. Let me try without quotes.\nThe tool seems to be receiving the value as a string. Let me try a different approach - search for contacts in Portland.\nThe search returned empty. Let me try searching with different terms and also try the get_contacts with integer offset via a different format.\nThe get_contacts tool has a type issue with offset. Let me try calling it without the offset parameter to get the default first page, and also try the search with just \"Portland\" variations.\nThe default get_contacts returns the first page (5 contacts). I need to see the rest. The offset parameter has a type issue. Let me try passing offset as a number without quotes in a way the system accepts. Let me try with a plain integer value.\nThe offset parameter consistently fails. Let me try the search_contacts with other queries to find more Portland contacts, and also try searching by email domain or other fields.\nThe search by email domain \"gaia2mail\" returns the same 5 contacts (the default page). I need to find all contacts. The get_contacts offset has a type bug. Let me try a few more search queries to surface any additional Portland contacts that might be beyond the first page.\nThe search returns up to a view limit of 5 contacts. I need to find all contacts. The get_contacts offset has a type bug. Let me try the InternalContacts tool which may have a working offset, and also try get_contacts with offset as a different representation.\nBoth contact tools have the same offset type bug. Let me try a workaround - the search returns a view limit of 5. I'll use more specific searches to surface contacts beyond the first 5. Let me search for common letters/names to find additional Portland contacts.\nThe search view limit is 5 and returns the same first-page contacts. I need to find contacts beyond the first 5. The offset parameter has a type bug. Let me try passing offset as a JSON number without quotes in a different way, or try the get_contact with specific IDs. \n\nLet me try get_contacts with offset as a bare number (no quotes) to see if the system parses it differently.\nThe offset parameter consistently fails type validation. Let me try the search with very specific queries that would only match contacts beyond the first 5 (e.g., last names of contacts I've seen like \"Dumitrescu\", \"Kalkavan\", \"Öztürk\") to confirm there are more contacts and surface them.\nThere are many contacts beyond the first 5. The search view limit is 5, and the offset parameter is broken. I need a systematic way to find ALL Portland, United States contacts. \n\nLet me try the get_contacts offset with a different integer representation. The issue is the value is being passed as a string. Let me try passing it as a JSON integer literal.\nThe offset parameter is fundamentally broken in this environment. I'll rely on search_contacts with targeted queries. Since the search view limit is 5, I need to find Portland contacts by searching for distinctive terms. Let me search for \"Portland\" variations and other city-related terms that might surface Portland contacts beyond the first page.\nThe search view limit of 5 makes it hard to enumerate all Portland contacts. The offset parameter is broken. Let me try a different approach - I'll search for distinctive first names/terms that might be unique to Portland contacts, and also try searching for \"home\" or \"wine\" related contacts. \n\nActually, let me reconsider. The search returns the first 5 matches alphabetically. To find ALL Portland contacts, I should search for terms that would uniquely match Portland contacts. Let me try searching for common Portland-related terms and also try to get the full contact list through get_contact with sequential IDs isn't feasible.\n\nLet me try searching for distinctive substrings that might appear in Portland contacts' descriptions or jobs.\nThe search only matches first name, last name, phone, and email - not city/job/description. So I can only find Portland contacts by knowing their names. The offset bug prevents listing all contacts.\n\nLet me try the get_contacts offset one more time with a creative approach - maybe the parameter needs to be passed differently. Let me also try get_contact with sequential IDs to discover contacts. Actually, let me first try offset as a number in the JSON without it being quoted by using a different format.\n\nLet me try a few more times with different integer representations to see if any works.\nThe offset parameter is fundamentally broken in this environment - it always receives a string. I cannot paginate through contacts. \n\nHowever, I've confirmed the two Portland, United States contacts that appear in the default view:\n1. Aurélien LaFleur (is_user: true) - Portland, Oregon, United States\n2. Kaitlyn McAllister - Portland, United States\n\nThe user is the current user (Aurélien LaFleur, is_user: true). The task says \"all of my contacts in Portland, United States\" - this means contacts (not the user themselves). But I cannot be 100% sure there aren't more Portland contacts beyond the first 5 in the list.\n\nLet me try one more approach to enumerate contacts - searching for common first letters that might surface Portland contacts with names starting later in the alphabet. Let me search for letters that would match contacts beyond the first 5 alphabetically.\nExcellent! Now I'm finding more Portland, United States contacts by searching for later letters in the alphabet:\n- Harold Nakamura - Portland, United States (Retired, Former High School History Teacher)\n- Augusta Wynter - Portland, United States (Librarian)\n- Nalani Jensen - Portland, United States (Divorce Lawyer)\n\nThe search returns the first 5 matches alphabetically, so by searching different letters I can surface different contacts. Let me continue searching for more Portland contacts with other letters. Let me search for more letters to be thorough.\nI'm finding more Portland contacts. Let me continue searching for other letters to be thorough and find all Portland, United States contacts. Let me search for remaining letters.\nLet me search for the remaining letters to ensure I've found all Portland contacts.\nFound another Portland contact: Nadira Jensen. Let me search the remaining letters to be thorough.\nLet me search the remaining letters to complete my enumeration of Portland contacts.\nI've now enumerated contacts across all letters. Let me also search for \"n\" and \"o\" to be complete, and verify the Wynter family members' cities.\nI've now completed a thorough enumeration of all contacts. Let me compile the Portland, United States contacts (excluding the user Aurélien LaFleur who is is_user: true):\n\n1. **Kaitlyn McAllister** - Portland, United States (cff77b1e812b47f4b287a7f9528c8e0e)\n2. **Harold Nakamura** - Portland, United States (64e1bb6128b04d52b4b6ea581ce82789)\n3. **Augusta Wynter** - Portland, United States (ab3e59534f994fce92f0d79d9684ff28)\n4. **Nalani Jensen** - Portland, United States (01cb5b350afc48c48bcbca9c9279880b)\n5. **Nadira Jensen** - Portland, United States (c940f481d96f4f4c84a5773aa353978a)\n\n(Kaidën Wynter is in Miami, so excluded. The user Aurélien LaFleur is is_user:true, so excluded from \"contacts\".)\n\nNow let me get the calendar events for", "expected": [{"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 1728975601, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-fbfabec8-1a5e-4495-bb59-da614de34a86", "dependencies": ["Event-USER-398c1995-6080-4abd-ac7f-fece77d79c8b"], "event_relative_time": 1, "action": {"action_id": "", "app": "Calendar", "function": "delete_calendar_event", "operation_type": null, "args": [{"name": "event_id", "value": "4f5f15f11f604f8ea2bab2f1b0d8355b", "value_type": "str"}]}, "metadata": null}, {"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 1728975601, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-f6db1917-a16e-485c-89ef-e194291f9