ckholm apartments. Let me check the crime API limit first since I'll need it.\nGood, I have 100 API calls available and 0 used. Now I need to make sure I have ALL Stockholm apartments. The search was truncated. Let me try searching with a high max_price as an integer to get the full list.\nThe tool is receiving the value as a string. Let me try without max_price but I need the full list. The search result keeps truncating. Let me try a different approach - search by each Stockholm zip code or just rely on the list_all. Let me get the full list_all_apartments output which may show more Stockholm entries. Actually, the list_all was also truncated. \n\nLet me think - the search returned 5 Stockholm apartments but was cut off mid-way through \"Stockholm City Living\". There might be more Stockholm apartments after it. Let me try searching with min_price to find higher-priced ones, or search by specific property names. \n\nActually, let me try the search again but this time the truncation happened at the 5th item. Let me see if there are more by searching with a min_price filter to skip the cheaper ones.\nThe numeric parameters are being passed as strings. Let me try a different approach to find all Stockholm apartments. Let me search by name patterns or check if there are more. Let me try searching with just a name filter that might catch Stockholm properties.\n\nActually, the issue is the tool wrapper. Let me try passing the integer differently. Let me retry the search and see if the truncation is consistent - maybe there are exactly 5 Stockholm apartments and the truncation is just display. But \"Stockholm City Living\" was cut off mid-property, suggesting there are more entries after it in the dict.\n\nLet me try to get the full list by searching with a name that would match. Let me search for \"Stockholm\" as a name.\nThere are more Stockholm apartments! I found \"Stockholm Waterfront\" (25000) and \"Stockholm Söder\" (22000) which are much more expensive. The list 