ally.\nThe tree output is also truncated at ~40 entries. The files are 0 bytes so I can't read content. I need to find the Copa América file by name. The naming patterns include \"daily_chronicle_news\", \"metro_herald\", and possibly others. Let me search for files with \"copa\" or \"america\" in the name. Since the tree truncates, let me try reading specific candidate files. The Copa América 2024 file likely has \"copa\" or \"america\" in its name. Let me try several naming patterns.\nThe tree truncates at ~40 entries, so there are more files I can't see. The Copa file must be among the unseen ones. Since the tree output is limited, let me try to read the full directory listing by reading the tree with a different approach. The issue is the display limit. Let me try reading the tree from a deeper level or check if there's a way to see all. \n\nActually, the tree shows files in sorted order. The visible ones go up to \"daily_chronicle_ne...\" (truncated). There are clearly many more. Let me try to find the Copa file by reading files with names I can guess. The pattern seems to be newspaper_name + years + number. Copa América might be in a file like \"sports_...\" or similar. \n\nLet me try a different strategy - read the tree but it keeps truncating. Let me check if there's a file listing tool that shows everything. The ls truncates at 10, tree at ~40. Let me try reading the tree output more - maybe I can page through. Actually the tree always starts from the same point. \n\nLet me think about this differently. The files are 0 bytes, so the \"content\" is the filename. The Copa América file is identified by its name containing \"copa\" or \"america\". Let me try reading more files systematically. Let me try common sports news naming.\nI need to see the complete file list. The tree truncates at ~40 entries. Let me try to get the full listing by reading the tree from a subdirectory or by checking the count. The files appear sorted alphabetically. The visible ones are all \"daily_chronicle_news_*\". There must be other prefixes (like \"metro_herald_*\" seen earlier, and possibly \"copa_*\" or \"sports_*\"). \n\nThe tree output is limited. Let me try to read the tree but it always shows the same first 40. Let me think - maybe I can read the directory in a way that shows all. Let me try the ls with detail to see if it shows more, or check if there's pagination. Actually, the ls showed 10 and tree shows ~40. \n\nLet me try reading the tree and see if it's actually complete or if there's a way to get more. The truncation at \"daily_chronicle_ne\" suggests there are more \"daily_chronicle_news\" files. The Copa file would be alphabetically after \"daily_chronicle\" if it starts with a letter after 'd', or it could be a different prefix. \n\nLet me try to find ", "expected": [{"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 1728975601.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-7d84d0fe-ef3e-485f-aa6d-511a9a3c726f", "dependencies": ["Event-USER-a188eb04-ab0a-435c-86b1-d8d8e3bb7b54"], "event_relative_time": 1.0, "action": {"action_id": "", "app": "Messages", "function": "send_message", "operation_type": null, "args": [{"name": "user_id", "value": "+3613812345678", "value_type": "str"}, {"name": "content", "value": "This is the news I was talking about.", "value_type": "str"}, {"name": "attachment_path", "value": "/Documents/news/metro_herald_833.txt", "value_type": "Optional[str]"}]}, "metadata": null}, {"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 1728975601.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-3d6ded6e-287e-4256-865a-4e9b78379e55", "dependencies": ["Event-USER-a188eb04-ab0a-435c-86b1-d8d8e3bb7b54"], "event_relative_time": 1.0, "action": {"action_id": "", "app": "Messages", "function": "send_message", "operation_type": null, "args": [{"name": "user_id", "value": "+46202123456", "value_type": "str"}, {"name": "content", "value": "This is the news I was talking about.", "value_type": "str"}, {"name": "attachmen