{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T11:28:11.168185", "benchmark": "gaia2", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia2_scenario_universe_21_4r8xq0", "task_desc": "I just ended things with my ex, the marketing manager I work with who also lives in Stockholm, and I want to cut all ties. Delete his contact, remove him from any Messages and Chats conversations we are both in, and move all emails sent to or received from him to the trash. Unsave the property located at the address we currently share, since I won't be living there anymore.", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: I just ended things with my ex, the marketing manager I work with who also lives in Stockholm, and I want to cut all ties. Delete his contact, remove \nAnswer given then (unverified): I'll help you cut all ties with your ex. Let me start by gathering information about him. First, let me find the contact and the shared property.\nThe search for\nActions used:  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  ->  -> ", "response": "I'll help you cut all ties with your ex. Let me start by gathering information about him across all the systems.\n\nFirst, let me find the contact and the shared property.\nThe search for \"Stockholm\" returned no contacts. Let me search differently and look at all contacts. Let me also check the full saved apartments list since it was truncated.\nThe offset needs to be an integer. Let me get the contacts list and the full saved apartments list.\nThe tool seems to be receiving the offset as a string. Let me try without specifying offset.\nThere are more contacts. Let me get the next page of contacts to find the ex (marketing manager in Stockholm).\nThe offset parameter has a type issue when passed as a string. Let me try searching by likely names/keywords instead. The ex is a marketing manager. Let me search for \"marketing\" and also try to find Stockholm-based contacts.\nThe search only matches name/phone/email. Let me look at the full contacts list by paginating. The offset issue is with string type. Let me try a different approach - search by common first names or just get more contacts. Let me try the offset with an integer value passed differently.\nThe tool consistently rejects string offsets. Let me try searching for the ex by his likely characteristics. Since he's a marketing manager who lives in Stockholm and works with Astrid, let me search by \"Stockholm\" again (returned nothing) and try other approaches. Let me search by partial name matches - maybe his name. Let me try common Swedish male names or search the email domain.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The search returned nothing for \"Stockholm\" but Astrid's contact has city_living Stockholm. The search only matches first name, last name, phone, email. So I need to find him by name. Let me try to get all contacts by paginating with a workaround. Let me try offset as a number without quotes issue - perhaps the function wrapper needs int. Let me try searching for likely names.\n\nLet me think about who could be the ex. He's a marketing manager, lives in Stockholm, works with Astrid. Let me search by email domain or try listing more contacts. Let me try the get_contacts with various integer offsets - maybe the issue is the JSON serialization. Let me just try a few name searches.\nThe search matches email domain. I need to find the ex. Let me look at the Messages and Chats conversations, which may reveal his name/phone. Let me also try to get more contacts. Let me list recent conversations in Messages and Chats.\nThese list tools also have the int offset issue. Let me try without offset parameter (defaults to 0).\nThe integer type enforcement is strict. The issue is the JSON serialization is passing numbers as strings. Let me try passing the integer values in a way that's recognized. Perhaps I need to not quote them. Let me retry with the offset as a bare integer.\nThe tool wrapper is converting to string. Let me try the search functions instead, which take string queries and may help me find the ex. Let me search Messages and Chats for \"Stockholm\" or \"marketing\" to find conversations involving him.\nThese searches return many conversations. I need to identify the ex specifically. The key identifiers: marketing manager, lives in Stockholm, works with Astrid (the user). Let me find his contact first. The contacts list tool has an int offset bug, but search works on name/phone/email. \n\nLet me think - the ex is a contact. I need his contact_id. Let me try to get the contacts list by trying the get_contacts with offset as a proper integer. The error says it got a string. This might be a serialization issue I can't control. \n\nAlternative: search contacts by his email or name. I don't know his name. But maybe I can find him through conversations. Let me read some conversations to find the marketing manager in Stockholm. Actually, a better approach: the ex is a contact, and conversations involve phone numbers (Messages) or internal user IDs (Chats). \n\nLet me try get_contacts with offset=0 again but maybe the issue is only when I pass it. Let me try passing offset as integer literal without it being quoted - the harness may handle it. Actually it already failed. \n\nLet me instead search contacts by first name fragments. The ex works with Astrid as a marketing manager. Let me search common names. Actually, let me reconsider - maybe I can find him via the saved apartment's shared address. The shared address is in Stockholm. Let me find which saved apartment is in Stockholm.\n\nFrom the saved apartments I've seen: Helsingborg, Uppsala (x2), Jönköping, Lund. None in Stockholm yet. The list was truncated. Let me get the full saved apartments list. The list_saved_apartments doesn't take offset, so it should return all. But the result was truncated in display. Let me look more carefully - I need to find the Stockholm one. Let me re-call and look at full output.\n\nActually, the truncation is just in my view. Let me think about the ex's identity differently. Let me search contacts by his job-related terms won't work. \n\nLet me try a different tactic: get all contacts by paginating. The offset bug - maybe I should pass it as a number type. Let me try once more with a clean integer.</think:6124c78e>I need to identify the ex. The contacts list tool has an integer-type issue with offset. Let me try to retrieve contacts by trying the offset as a numeric value, and also re-examine the saved apartments to find the Stockholm one. Let me try get_contacts with offset 0 again carefully.\nThe get_contacts tool consistently rejects the offset. Let me work around this by searching contacts by name fragments. The ex is a marketing manager in Stockholm who works with Astrid. Let me try searching for likely first names and also look at the saved apartments fully to find the Stockholm shared address.\n\nLet me re-call list_saved_apartments to see the full list (it may show the Stockholm one).\nThe saved apartments list is truncated. I need to find the Stockholm one. Let me search saved apartments by location Stockholm.\nThe bool parameter also has a type issue. Let me try without saved_only, searching all apartments in Stockholm.\nI found a saved Stockholm apartment: \"Stockholm Haven\" (Södermalm, Stockholm), apartment_id `5a8070b881fb4a07bbeffc8b1e9b0109`, saved: true. This is likely the shared address. But I should verify it's the one they \"currently share.\" Let me check the full saved apartments list to confirm there's only one Stockholm saved apartment. The list_saved_apartments was truncated. Let me get the complete list by checking each. Actually, the search with saved_only failed due to bool type. \n\nLet me reconsider - I need to be careful. The task says \"the property located at the address we currently share.\" There may be exactly one saved Stockholm apartment. From the saved list I saw: Helsingborg, Uppsala (x2), Jönköping, Lund, and truncated. The Stockholm Haven appears in the all-Stockholm search as saved:true. Let me confirm by getting the full saved list. The truncation cut off after \"Uppsala Oasis\". Let me try to get more saved apartments - but list_saved_apartments returns all at once (truncated in display). \n\nLet me focus on identifying the ex first, then handle the apartment. The ex is a contact. I still need his contact_id. Let me try to find him through conversations. Let me read some of the \"marketing\" search result conversations in Messages to find the ex's phone number, then map to contact.\n\nActually, a more efficient approach: the ex is a contact with job \"Marketing Manager\" and city_living \"Stockholm\". The search_contacts only matches name/phone/email. But I can find him via conversations. Let me read conversations that mention \"marketing manager\" or look at the participants.\n\nLet me read a few of the marketing-related Messages conversations. But there are 13. Let me instead try to find the ex by reading conversations and looking for a Stockholm marketing manager. \n\nAlternatively, let me reconsider the get_contacts offset bug. Mayb", "expected": [{"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 1728975601.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-2a831ac0-6c73-4ab7-abb4-dca91d2ce09c", "dependencies": ["Event-USER-bd5021a2-d4ab-458a-b76f-a36fc820164a"], "event_relative_time": 1.0, "action": {"action_id": "", "app": "Contacts", "function": "delete_contact", "operation_type": null, "args": [{"name": "contact_id", "value": "eabcdec881124416952bcfebbc179285", "value_type": "str"}]}, "metadata": null}, {"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 1728975601.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-97ccce4c-cbe1-4399-aba2-8b4988566350", "dependencies": ["Event-USER-bd5021a2-d4ab-458a-b